#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""publish-public — compute and (eventually) push the public mirror of Evolve.

Evolve develops in the private ``cjalden/evolve`` repo and is published as a
FRESH PUBLIC REPO at ``evolve-ops/evolve``. There is NO history rewrite: the
private repo stays the dev origin and is never scrubbed; only the *published
output* is gated. This tool computes that output and proves it is clean.

The cut line is PUBLIC-BY-DEFAULT minus an explicit private denylist for
everything outside ``docs/``, and PRIVATE-BY-DEFAULT for ``docs/`` (PD-3b,
2026-08-15 — the 08-15 leak showed a denylist cannot win the race against
newly-invented document classes): a ``docs/`` path ships only if it matches
the manifest's ``public_docs`` allowlist or is a publish-time sanitize
target, and the ``private`` denylist wins on conflict, always:

    public tree = (git ls-files at HEAD)
                  − (paths matching ``private``)
                  − (docs/ paths matching neither ``public_docs`` nor a
                     tools/publish_sanitize.py target)

Two gates run on the PUBLIC SUBSET (never the whole repo):

  1. The denylist (docs/public-manifest.yaml) removes private paths.
  2. The reserved-token scrub reuses the launch-scrub module's token list
     and per-line scanner (packages/admin/tests/test_public_launch_scrub.py)
     so they cannot drift, but DECOUPLES the path exemptions: it scans
     every shipped file and exempts ONLY the explicit, minimal
     ``PUBLIC_OUTPUT_EXEMPTIONS`` set defined below — NOT the private-CI
     ``ALLOWLISTED_PATHS`` (whose job is to keep token-bearing test
     fixtures from reddening per-PR CI, and which must never silently
     greenlight a public leak). PASS therefore means "no reserved tokens
     in any shipped file outside that named set."

Between them, a publish-time sanitizer (tools/publish_sanitize.py, PUBLIC-2)
rewrites the few load-bearing dev-apparatus files (CLAUDE.md, .claude/settings.json,
the cutover runbook, .mailmap, PLACEHOLDER_NAMING.md) in the public output only — the
private repo keeps their real values. The scrub therefore scans WHAT WOULD SHIP.

Default mode is ``--dry-run``: it makes NO network calls and NO commits. It
prints the public file count + sample, the full excluded (private) list with
the glob that excluded each, the scrub result, and a WARN-level list of
shipped files that still reference denylisted paths (broken links in the
public tree — rewrite or accept each).

FORWARD-SYNC (PD-4, forward-sync v1, operator-approved 2026-07-02; AUTOMATED
2026-07-29). The first snapshot was pushed 2026-07-01. Every subsequent
release is a republish under **Model A**: the public repo is always exactly
ONE squashed commit. Each ``--execute --target <url> --push`` builds a fresh
``git init`` tree sharing no history with the remote and FORCE-pushes it —
the force is the designed behavior, not an override. No public history
accumulates.

Since 2026-07-29 the loop runs in CI on every push to ``main``
(``.github/workflows/publish-public.yml``); the sequence below is the
break-glass/manual path and the canonical definition of what the workflow
does (docs/runbook-public-cutover.md, PD-4):

    tools/publish-public --status                  # drift since last publish (no network)
    tools/publish-public --dry-run                 # must end SCRUB: PASS (exit 0)
    tools/publish-public --execute --target <url> --push \
        --i-understand-this-publishes              # build, gate, force-push
    tools/publish-public --verify --target <url>   # fresh clone: token grep + 1-commit check
    # then manually close public issues fixed in this release

NOTE the mode dispatch order in main(): --status, then --verify, then
--execute. They are NOT composable — passing --verify in the same invocation
as --execute short-circuits to verify and NOTHING is published. Always run
--verify as a separate invocation.

CI adds ``--skip-if-unchanged`` to the --execute step so a push that changes
only manifest-denied paths does not churn the public commit sha; the check
compares the built tree's git tree-id against the published one, which cannot
lose a commit the way a "did this push touch a public path" filter can.

On a successful push the tool records a provenance marker at
``docs/private/last-public-release.json`` (gitignored — never ships) so the
next ``--status`` can report how far the public snapshot is behind.

DRIFT DETECTION (``--check-drift``, 2026-08-11). The republish automation can
stop republishing while every run still reports SUCCESS — its secret gate skips
green when ``PUBLIC_REPO_PUSH_TOKEN`` is absent, which is deliberate but was
silent (32 green runs, 12 days stale). ``--check-drift`` is the outcome-based,
cause-agnostic answer: it builds the tree this checkout WOULD publish and
compares it to what IS published, so a missing secret, a revoked token, a
failed push, or an out-of-band force-push all surface identically. It reads the
published HEAD **unauthenticated** (evolve-ops/evolve is public since
2026-07-28) — a detector must never need the credential whose absence it
detects. Scheduled by ``.github/workflows/publish-drift.yml``.

Spec: docs/spec-public-2026-06-18.md §4.

Run:
    tools/publish-public                 # dry-run (default)
    tools/publish-public --dry-run       # explicit
    tools/publish-public --json          # dry-run, machine-readable
    tools/publish-public --status        # drift vs the last published release
    tools/publish-public --check-drift --target <url>   # is the SNAPSHOT current?
"""
